</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Which one appears to be the bounciest? Which one appears to be the least bouncy? Be prepared to show your
        reasoning. </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>The second ball is the bounciest. The balls were all dropped from the same height, and its bounces are the highest.
    The third ball is the least bouncy. </para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> For each one, write an equation expressing the bounce height in terms of the bounce number \(n\). </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answers:</para>
  
  <list>
    <item> Ball 1: \(a=150 \cdot (\frac{1}{2})^n\) or \(a=150 \cdot (0.5)^n\) </item>
    <item> Ball 2: \(b=150 \cdot (\frac{7}{10})^n\) or \(b=150 \cdot (0.7)^n\) </item>
    <item> Ball 3: \(c=150 \cdot (\frac{2}{5})^n\) or \(c=150 (0.4)^n\)</item>
  </list>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> Explain how the equations could tell us which one is the most bouncy. </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answers:</para>
  
  <para>The bounciness of the ball is measured by the rebound factor, which is the base of the exponential expression. That
    is \(\frac12\) for the first ball, \(\frac{7}{10}\) for the second ball, and \(\frac25\) for the third ball. The
    larger this number, the bouncier the ball.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> If the bounciest one were dropped from a height of 300 cm, what equation would model its bounce height \(h\)?
      </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para> \(h=300 \cdot (\frac{7}{10})^n\) </para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>


